  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe low pressure system that brought showers the previous days will \ncontinue to exit off the east coast this morning and the low level \nclouds behind will begin to dissipate from west to east. The drier \nair in Eastern Indiana as been slow to move into the western \ncounties of the CWA this morning. As it does clear out, there will \nbe potential for patchy fog across Western Ohio this morning. Though \nhow far east it reaches is uncertain given the slow clearing and \nwill assess the need for an SPS over the next few hours. Afterwards, \nhigh pressure will nose in from the south and dry weather is \nexpected today. \n\nAn unsettled pattern begins late tonight into early Tuesday as a \nweak shortwave moves through the upper Great Lakes. Rain chances are \nlow currently and confined to Northern Ohio and Northwestern \nPennsylvania since there will be a decent amount of dry air to fight \nwith to the south. There will be warm air advecting into the region \nas flow shifts to be out of the west leading to some instability \ndeveloping across the region so thunderstorms will be possible if \nprecipitation does develop. Additionally, with the warm air \nadvection, temperatures will climb into the low to mid 80s across \nthe region Tuesday afternoon and will rival record high temperatures \nfor the day. The best areas to see records broken will be at most \nOhio climate sites with Youngstown staying a few degrees under and \nErie staying in the upper 70s. Record highs for the day can be found \nbelow in the climate section. \n\nThe better chance for widespread rain and thunderstorms will be late \nTuesday night into Wednesday as a cold front sweeps north to south \nacross the region. Given the timing of the front, instability will \nbe minimal so not anticipating much more than general thunderstorms \nat this point. This front will settle to the south across the Ohio \nValley during the day Wednesday. Rain chances will be lower during \nthe day, but will increase further south into Central Ohio. With \nexpected rain showers, temperatures on Wednesday will be cooler in \nthe low to upper 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCanadian high pressure briefly builds in from the north late \nWednesday into Thursday. A strong upper level trough begins to \ndevelop across the Canadian Prairies that will phase with a cut off \nlow located across the Southwestern CONUS. This trough will rapidly \ndeepen as it progresses eastward into the Great Lakes region late \nThursday. The associated surface low will have a similar \nstrengthening across the Great Lakes and a strong cold front will \nmove through on Friday. There's still quite a bit of model \ndiscrepancies with this system and how it phases with the cut off \nlow across the southwest. This leads to varying timing and strength \nof the upper level trough when it moves through the region. The GFS \nhas trended slower than both the Euro and Canadian models and has \nbeen slightly deeper with colder air advecting in behind. \n\nRegardless, this set up with bring in strong winds beginning Friday \nmorning into Saturday with rain showers expected during that time \nframe as well. Additionally, this will usher in the coldest \ntemperatures of the season so far for Saturday and Sunday mornings \nwith chilly wind chills as well due to the gusty winds. How cold\nit will get is uncertain due to the reasoning mentioned above \nwith model guidance. Lingering lake effect showers are likely \nacross Northeastern Ohio and Northwestern Pennsylvania Saturday \ninto early Sunday. Will definitely be a system to watch how it \ndevelops as the need for wind headlines will be possible. High \npressure builds into the region Sunday with dry weather expected\nand temperatures beginning to moderate.",
